<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<object type="traktor.mcp.SkillAsset">
	<name>event-set-effects</name>
	<description>Spawn effects/sound/decals on an entity by raising named events from script via an EventSetComponent.</description>
	<whenToUse><![CDATA[Asked to spawn a particle effect (blood, sparks, dust), sound, or decal at an entity on a gameplay trigger (hit, death, pickup) — fired on demand from a LUA script.]]></whenToUse>
	<body><![CDATA[# Spawn effects via an EventSetComponent

Attach a set of *named* entity events to an entity, then `raise` one by name from script to spawn a transient effect/sound/decal at that entity on a gameplay trigger (being hit, dying, picking up). The spawned instance is managed by the world; a non-looping effect ends by itself.

## Author the component (on the entity asset, not the scene reference)
Add a `traktor.world.EventSetComponentData` to the entity's `components`. Its `eventData` member is a **map** of `name -> IEntityEventData`:

```
set_instance_member { guid:<entity>, member:"components", append:true, value:[
  { "$type":"traktor.world.EventSetComponentData", "set": {
      "eventData": {
        "{{event}}": { "$type":"traktor.spray.SpawnEffectEventData", "set": {
            "effect":"<Effect guid>", "follow": true, "useRotation": true } }
      } } } ] }
```

- **Put it on the shared entity asset** (e.g. `Entities/Humanoid/Humanoid`), not on an `ExternalEntityData` placement in the scene. The component then travels with every instance of that entity.
- `SpawnEffectEventData` fields:
  - `effect` (`Id<Effect>`);
  - `transform`: offset, identity by default;
  - `follow`: the effect tracks the entity as it moves;
  - `useRotation`: orient by the entity's rotation.
- Build the entity asset after editing.

### Other event types (same map, different `$type`)
- `traktor.spray.SoundEventData`: play a sound.
- `traktor.world.DecalEventData`: project a decal.
- `traktor.spray.EnvelopeFeedbackEventData` / `traktor.spray.OscillateFeedbackEventData`: camera, image-process or UI feedback such as a shake. There is no rumble.

Any number of named entries can share one `EventSetComponentData`.

## Raise from script
```lua
-- resolve & cache once (component lives on the entity)
self._events = entity:getComponent(traktor.world.EventSetComponent)
...
self._events:raise("{{event}}")            -- spawn at the entity
self._events:raise("{{event}}", offset)    -- spawn at a Transform offset
```
- `raise` is edge/one-shot: each call spawns **one** fresh instance. For a continuous effect (e.g. bleeding while attacked) call it on a timer (every ~0.3 s) for as long as the condition holds, letting the effect's own duration overlap.
- It returns an `IEntityEventInstance` (or `nil`). You can `:cancelImmediate()` / `:cancelEnd()` it, but usually just let it finish.

## Gotchas (hard-won)
- **A spawned effect only ends when it finishes, and a looping effect never does.** The cloned Spray examples loop (`loopEnd` 1), so set `loopEnd = loopStart` (e.g. both 0) for event effects, or keep the returned instance and `cancelEnd()` it. Otherwise every raise leaks a live instance into the 512 cap.
- **`raise` needs an `EventManagerComponent` in the world**, or it silently returns `nil` and nothing spawns. The engine's `World` adds one automatically (`EventManagerComponent(512)`), so it works out of the box. But 512 is the max number of concurrent instances: don't raise every frame.
- **Prefer this over a permanent `EffectComponentData`** when the effect is event-driven. An `EffectComponent` is enabled by default and auto-plays once on spawn (a stray puff at scene start). An event spawns strictly on `raise`, with no initial play.
- The spawned effect inherits the entity's transform. Bake any height offset into the effect's *source position* (or pass a `raise` offset) rather than moving the entity.
- Effect authoring: *particle-effect*. Script side (`import(traktor)`, direct component types, `getComponent`): *traktor-lua-scripting*.]]></body>
	<engineVersion/>
	<published>true</published>
	<parameters>
		<item type="traktor.mcp.SkillParameter">
			<name>event</name>
			<description>Event name raised from script</description>
			<defaultValue>Hit</defaultValue>
		</item>
	</parameters>
</object>
